insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('09ceaf13-f2e8-5523-85cb-62edce828c64'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5)',E'**CHAPTER 1  \n**\n\n**LEGAL MANDATE, DRRM GOVERNANCE AND  \nINSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY**\n\n**  \n**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n*  \nAssessment-informed working draft  \nSubject to institutional and legal validation*\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Document attribute** | **Control information**                                                                                                                                          |\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document title         | Ormoc City CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations – Chapter 1                                                                                                           |\n| Chapter title          | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability                                                                                                  |\n| Document status        | Working draft for technical and institutional validation                                                                                                         |\n| Document owner         | City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office                                                                                                               |\n| Technical custodian    | CDRRMO Head / designated MOO focal person                                                                                                                        |\n| Reviewing offices      | City Legal Office; Office of the City Mayor; Sangguniang Panlungsod Committee on Disaster Management; CPDO; CDRRMO; OCD Region VIII; other offices as applicable |\n| Version / date         | Draft 1.2 / 6 November 2026                                                                                                                                      |\n| Approval authority     | To be determined under the City''s document-control and approval arrangements                                                                                     |\n| Review cycle           | At least annually and following material changes in law, governance arrangements or operational requirements                                                     |\n\n# REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**   | **Description**                                                                                                                                                                                          | **Prepared / reviewed by** | **Approval** |\n|-------------|------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------|\n| 1.0         | Oct 2026   | Revised assessment-based working draft (IAT v1.3)                                                                                                                                                        | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.1         | 6 Nov 2026 | Updated to IAT v1.5: October 2026 FGD, KIIs and site visits; 2021–2024 City issuances; revised findings, priorities and action plan. Restyled to the Chapter 2 design.                                   | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n| 1.2         | 6 Nov 2026 | Added staff perspectives on planning and budgeting (1.4), the scenario-based EOC structure (6.3), the Brgy. Liloan site visit, the EOC geospatial reference point, and APA-captioned figures and tables. | Project drafting team      | For review   |\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nDisaster risk reduction and management (DRRM) in Ormoc City requires an integrated institutional system that connects executive leadership, policy direction, technical management, operational coordination and community-level action. Its effectiveness depends not only on the existence of the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), but also on the clarity of their respective mandates, decision-making authority, functional relationships, accountability arrangements and operational interfaces.\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, governance and institutional accountability framework for the City''s CDRRMO and EOC. It defines how the City Government organizes, directs, coordinates, monitors and continuously improves its DRRM functions across normal operations, preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response and recovery.\n\nThe chapter adopts a systems-thinking perspective: disaster risk is understood as the consequence of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, institutional capacities and dependencies across social, economic, environmental and physical systems. Accordingly, the City''s DRRM governance arrangements must operate as an integrated system rather than as a collection of independent offices, committees and emergency procedures.\n\nThe chapter also applies Total Quality Management (TQM) principles to institutional operations. These include process ownership, evidence-based decision-making, stakeholder participation, standardization, performance measurement, corrective action and continuous improvement.\n\nThe institutional provisions are informed by the **Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.7 (workbook v1-7, 11 November 2026)**, particularly its nine assessment sheets.\n\nThe assessment is treated as a diagnostic baseline, not as a substitute for legal compliance verification or formal institutional approval.\n\n## 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities\n\nThe October 2026 assessment identifies a significant distinction between the maturity of the CDRRMO, the EOC and their institutional interface.\n\n**Figure 1.1**\n\n*Institutional Maturity and Mandatory Compliance, IAT v1.7*\n\n![Figure 1: 1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities](/library/ch1v12/fig-01.webp)\n\n*Note.* Maturity index on a 0–4 scale. The Institutional Maturity Index (IMI) combines Parts A and B; Part C (institutional interface) is reported separately. Mandatory compliance is the share of scored mandatory indicators rated 3 or 4. Source: Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.5, Dashboard.\n\nThe results demonstrate that the CDRRMO has a stronger institutional foundation than the EOC, while the interface between the two remains the weakest component. This is a critical systems issue: the existence of functioning CDRRMO activities does not automatically translate into a coordinated, documented and reliable EOC operating system.\n\nThe dashboard identifies 29 high-priority gaps: 17 in the CDRRMO assessment (Part A) and 12 in the EOC assessment (Part B). The separate Part C assessment adds 2 high-priority interface gaps that need action by other City Government offices and external partners.\n\nFor this chapter, the most consequential findings are:\n\n**Table 1.1**\n\n*Most Consequential Assessment Findings for Governance (IAT v1.7)*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 52%" />\n<col style="width: 32%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Assessment reference</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Finding (IAT v1.7)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Governance implication</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>A1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No ordinance establishes the CDRRMO as a regular department with the JMC 2014-1 divisions. An Executive Order is the only basis.</td>\n<td>The legal and organizational foundation needs an enabling ordinance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>A1.2 – Score 3</td>\n<td>EO 2024-032A names all RA 10121 Sec. 11 seats, including five CSO and two private-sector seats. Gaps: the academe seat was dropped; the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024, has no amending EO; SP accreditation of the CSOs is not shown; the third private-sector seat is vacant.</td>\n<td>Issue an amending EO and keep a yearly membership register.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.3 – Score 3</td>\n<td>Meetings and resolutions are on file for 2024–2025, but not for four consecutive quarters. The presiding officer changes from meeting to meeting without a written designation by the Chair.</td>\n<td>Keep quarterly meetings; file minutes and attendance each quarter; designate the presiding officer in writing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>A1.4 – Score 3</td>\n<td>The CDRRMO acts as Secretariat, but no written secretariat procedure exists. Records lapses: an undated resolution, a reused control number, missing certifications, and two 2022 resolutions filed under each other''s numbers.</td>\n<td>Formalize the Secretariat SOP and a resolution register (GOV-02).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.5 – Score 3</td>\n<td>Evacuation and class-suspension rules are applied event by event (CDRRMC Res. Nos. 08 and 09 s.2021 for Typhoon Odette; Res. No. 06 s.2022 for TD Agaton). The evacuation ordinance lacks procedural steps, and the Zoning Ordinance has no hazard overlay zones.</td>\n<td>Link ordinances to warning levels and risk information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>A1.6 – Score 3</p>\n<p>(Medium)</p></td>\n<td>The State of Calamity process has been used (SP Res. No. 2021-366, Odette; CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024, dengue), but there is no stand-alone written protocol, and Quick Response Fund resolutions do not cite the declaration relied on.</td>\n<td>Standardize declaration, QRF use and lifting (GOV-04).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>A1.7 – Score 3</td>\n<td>DRRM takes part in the LFC review and the CDP. No CDC or LFC minutes show CDRRMO input changing a decision.</td>\n<td>Use the CDC and LFC seats to secure anticipatory-action and EOC funding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>A1.8 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No State of Imminent Disaster procedure, RDRRMC request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template exists.</td>\n<td>Establish the RA 12287 anticipatory governance process (GOV-05).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>A2.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>CDRRMO sections exist but not as an approved JMC 2014-1 structure; normal-operations and response tasks are mixed.</td>\n<td>Approve the permanent structure (Chapter 2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td><p>B1.1 – Score 2</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>No EO creates the EOC. Site visit (October 2026): a developing EOC, co-located with the OpCen in the CDRRM Office, runs 24-hour duty for City Traffic Monitoring, Public Safety and Emergency Monitoring.</td>\n<td>Issue an EO establishing the EOC and its relationship with the CDRRMC, the OpCen and ICS.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td><p>B6.1 – Score 1</p>\n<p>(High)</p></td>\n<td>The ICS–EOC interface and chain of command are not defined.</td>\n<td>Separate incident command from EOC coordination.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note.* Scores use the 0–4 IAT scale; priority is shown where it is High or Medium.\n\nThe assessment also identifies broader system dependencies involving City Planning, Engineering, Budget, Environment, Social Welfare, Health, Education, Utilities and other institutions. These are addressed through the interface arrangements established in this chapter and the succeeding operational modules.\n\n**Management implication**: The priority is not simply to create more committees or issue additional policies. It is to establish a coherent governance system in which each mandate has an accountable owner, each process has a defined workflow, each decision has a legitimate authority, and each institutional output can be verified.\n\n**Assessment interpretation**: The workbook''s Dashboard (IAT v1.7, 11 Nov 2026) reports 1.98/4.00 as the combined Institutional Maturity Index, after the evidence rule (Methodology 5.2) was applied to 15 indicators. Part C is reported separately and is not included in that index. Unscored indicators and provisional desk assessments must not be read as proof of compliance.\n\n## 1.2 Interpretation of institutional Maturity and Compliance\n\nThe assessment methodology distinguishes institutional maturity from mandatory compliance. A domain may achieve a relatively high maturity index while remaining legally non-compliant because one or more mandatory requirements have not attained the required rating.\n\nThis distinction is particularly important for Ormoc:\n\n- CDRRMO Legal Mandate and DRRM Governance (A1) has a maturity index of 2.47/4.00, but only 66.7% mandatory compliance.\n\n- EOC Legal Basis and Concept of Operations (B1) has a maturity index of 2.23/4.00, but only 20% mandatory compliance.\n\n- The overall mandatory compliance rate is 32.9%, against a combined maturity index of 1.98.\n\nThe implication is that institutional improvements must address both capability and compliance. A procedure that is practiced informally cannot automatically be treated as an established institutional control. Similarly, the existence of an executive order, organizational chart or operational facility does not establish compliance unless the relevant requirements are documented, approved, implemented and supported by evidence.\n\nThe assessment uses the following evidence-based maturity logic:\n\n| **Rating** | **Maturity classification**  | **Institutional interpretation**                                                             |\n|------------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0          | Absent                       | No established arrangement.                                                                  |\n| 1          | Initial / Ad hoc             | Practice depends on individuals and is not adequately documented.                            |\n| 2          | Developing                   | Arrangement exists but is incomplete, outdated, unapproved or inconsistently implemented.    |\n| 3          | Compliant / Established      | Applicable standards are met through approved documentation and demonstrated implementation. |\n| 4          | Advanced / Institutionalized | Compliance is sustained, tested, reviewed and improved through documented learning.          |\n\nFor purposes of this MOO, a procedure shall not be considered fully institutionalized merely because it appears in the Manual. Its implementation must be demonstrated through records, operational outputs, verification and periodic review.\n\n## 1.3 Assessment Methods and Evidence Base\n\nThe October 2026 assessment used four methods. Each indicator was scored under the evidence rule in Methodology 5.2: interviews alone support a maximum score of 2; a score of 3 needs an approved document and proof of use; a score of 4 adds a test, exercise or after-action review.\n\n**Table 1.2**\n\n*Assessment Methods Used in October–November 2026*\n\n| **Method**                  | **Coverage**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | **Use in this chapter**                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Focus group discussion      | CDRRMO Head and staff, held at the CDRRM Office, which houses the OpCen and the EOC. Indicators were scored together using the IAT workbook.                                                                                                                             | Baseline scores for A1, A2 and B1                                                                 |\n| Key informant interviews    | CDRRMO Chiefs of Operations, Research and Planning, and Administration; CPDO; CSWDO; City Administrator''s Office. Positions only, per RA 10173.                                                                                                                          | Roles, interfaces and decision rights                                                             |\n| Observation and site visits | EOC; Operations and Rescue Office; Brgy. Liloan, one of the flood-prone barangays, with an established evacuation site, evacuation protocol, and camp coordination and management arrangements.                                                                          | EOC status (B1.1), duty system (A2.5), evacuation and camp management practice (A1.5, A3.6, A8.5) |\n| Document review             | City plans (DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032, CDP 2024–2030, LCCAP, CDRA, CLUP); 23 City documents from 2023–2025; 48 City issuances from 2021–2024 (CDRRMC resolutions, Executive Orders, SP resolutions); the CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers (2026). | Legal basis, Council practice, declarations and funding decisions                                 |\n\n*Note.* Informants are identified by office only, per RA 10173.\n\n**Stakeholder position.** All offices consulted (CDRRMO, CPDO, CSWDO and the City Administrator''s Office) share one aim: clear roles and responsibilities for each unit and accountable person, and that these are carried out. All agree that a Manual of Operations will help significantly. This chapter responds by giving each governance function an accountable owner, a workflow and a record.\n\n**Issuance review (2021–2024).** The review found the following points relevant to governance:\n\n- **Council composition.** The CDRRMC was reorganized by EO No. 2022-0006 and later by EO Nos. 2023-0041 and 2024-032A. The membership register shall carry only the latest EO and its amendments.\n\n- **Warning-driven actions are decided event by event.** Pre-emptive evacuation (CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2021) and suspension of work and classes (Res. No. 08 s.2021; Res. No. 06 s.2022) were each adopted by a separate resolution at the time of the event. A pre-set warning–action matrix (A6.2) shall replace this.\n\n- **Declarations.** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366). The CDRRMC recommended a one-year State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak (Res. No. 06 s.2024). See Process 4 (GOV-04).\n\n- **Odette response decisions.** The Council approved a PHP 2 M standby fund for prepositioning food and non-food items (Res. No. 11 s.2021) and asked LEYECO V to run its 2 MW standby generator during widespread outages (Res. No. 10 s.2021). It also sent cash aid from the 2021 Quick Response Fund to other LGUs (SP Res. Nos. 2021-365, -367 and -369). No standing mutual-aid agreement exists (A10.1).\n\n- **Planning and funding.** The Council approved the DRRM 10-Year Plan (Res. No. 01 s.2023), and EO No. 2022-00020 created the DRRM Plan Technical Working Group. The flood control and drainage master plan was funded in 2023 (Res. Nos. 03, 04 and 07 s.2023; about PHP 11.0 M), but no output was sighted.\n\n- **Records control.** The files of CDRRMC Res. Nos. 03 and 04 s.2022 contain each other''s text; the CCCM Executive Order is filed as No. 263 but the scan reads 243; SP Res. No. 2021-357 still cites 110 barangays. These findings support GOV-02 and GOV-08.\n\n## 1.4 Staff Perspectives on DRRM Planning and Budgeting\n\nDuring the assessment, CDRRMO staff shared written reflections on how the office plans, budgets and coordinates, together with a proposal to strengthen the link with barangays (Chapter 2, section 10.2). The papers show a shared commitment to a risk-informed office. The assessment team read them alongside the City''s records, so that points the office already agrees on can be acted on now, and points that need more data can be settled together at the December 2026 validation workshop.\n\n**Table 1.3**\n\n*Themes Raised in Staff Reflections and What the Records Show*\n\n| **Theme**                                     | **What the records show**                                                                                                                                                                                     | **Agreed way forward**                                                        |\n|-----------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Balance of the budget across thematic areas   | The 2022 Trust Fund plan gave 20% of the 70% component to prevention and mitigation, 55% to preparedness and 25% to response (Figure 1.2). No thematic split is on file yet for 2024–2026.                    | Table the 2024–2026 LDRRMF split by thematic area at the next CDRRMC meeting. |\n| Use of risk data in decisions                 | A CDRA exists (EO No. 2023-0025) but is not yet carried into zoning, project screening or investment-plan tagging. There is no loss database.                                                                 | Score every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP (A3.2, C1.2).            |\n| Monitoring budget use against results         | No annual LDRRMP monitoring report or utilization report is on file. Trust Fund balances of PHP 79.5 M (2024) and PHP 52.7 M (2025) suggest slow use.                                                         | Start a quarterly results-and-finance report (A10.4, A4.4).                   |\n| Preparedness items that need funding          | Hazard-specific contingency plans, the Public Service Continuity Plan, this Manual, partnerships and school programs are all CDP 2024–2030 PPAs (2.4, 2.5, 2.10.3, 2.16). The gap is execution, not planning. | Track each PPA in the Action Plan.                                            |\n| Alignment with national and global frameworks | The DRRM 10-Year Plan follows the NDRRMC 2022 guidebook; the CDP adopts the UNDRR Ten Essentials. No LCCAP–LDRRMP crosswalk exists.                                                                           | Prepare the crosswalk with the CPDO.                                          |\n| Link between planning and budgeting           | No written budget cycle says who formulates and who executes the investment plan.                                                                                                                             | Adopt the budget-cycle RACI (Chapter 2, 2.16.2).                              |\n\n*Note.* Records reviewed: CDRRMC resolutions 2021–2025, CDP 2024–2030, DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 and the IAT v1.7 workbook. No record reviewed raises concerns about how any section handles funds. The City''s Full Disclosure postings (A10.5) and its model-LGU partnerships (EO No. 2023-0026) are strengths to build on.\n\n**Figure 1.2**\n\n*Allocation of the 70% Component of the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF by Thematic Area*\n\n![Figure 2: 1.4 Staff Perspectives on DRRM Planning and Budgeting](/library/ch1v12/fig-02.webp)\n\n*Note.* Data from the 2022 Trust Fund LDRRMF budget program and investment plan (CDRRMC Res. No. 01 s.2022). The response share funded COVID-19 vaccines; preparedness was mostly vehicles; prevention and mitigation was data equipment. Comparable splits for 2024–2026 are not yet available.\n\nThe following shared commitments are proposed for the office to confirm at the December 2026 workshop:\n\n- We will show how the LDRRMF splits across the four thematic areas, starting with 2024–2026, so that decisions rest on shared numbers.\n\n- Planning and execution need different strengths. Research and Planning brings risk evidence; Administration brings costing, procurement and audit discipline. The investment plan is strongest when both sign it.\n\n- Response readiness and long-term risk reduction both need funding. Scoring every proposal against the CDRA and the LDRRMP helps the office balance them.\n\n- Research and Planning will build one monitoring tool that every division reports into each quarter.\n\n- Research and Planning leads the formulation of the investment plan; Administration leads execution; the CDRRMO Head approves; the CDRRMC endorses.\n\n# 2. Purpose\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal and institutional governance arrangements governing the CDRRMO, CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nIt aims to:\n\n1.  Establish a clear and legally grounded division of authority among the Local Chief Executive, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n2.  Define the institutional responsibilities, decision rights and accountability of participating City Government offices.\n\n3.  Institutionalize transparent, inclusive and evidence-based DRRM governance.\n\n4.  Establish procedures for CDRRMC meetings, resolutions, recommendations and action monitoring.\n\n5.  Standardize the City''s procedures for State of Calamity and State of Imminent Disaster declarations.\n\n6.  Strengthen vertical and horizontal coordination among the City Government, barangays, national government agencies, neighbouring LGUs, CSOs, private-sector partners and humanitarian organizations.\n\n7.  Establish process ownership, responsibility assignments and decision-making controls using the RACI framework.\n\n8.  Embed risk-informed governance, quality assurance, performance monitoring and continuous improvement into institutional operations.\n\n# 3. Scope and Application\n\nThis chapter applies to the following institutional actors:\n\n- Office of the City Mayor;\n\n- CDRRMC and its Secretariat;\n\n- CDRRMO Head and functional divisions;\n\n- EOC Manager and EOC functional sections;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod;\n\n- City Government departments, offices and units;\n\n- Barangay DRRMCs in all 85 barangays;\n\n- National government agencies and regional DRRM institutions;\n\n- Accredited CSOs, ACDVs, private-sector organizations and other partners.\n\nIts provisions apply under three operating conditions:\n\n| **Operating condition**                       | **Institutional focus**                                                                                                                                | **Primary governance mechanism**                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Normal operations                             | Risk reduction, institutional planning, preparedness, capacity development, monitoring and continuity.                                                 | CDRRMC oversight and CDRRMO management.                                           |\n| Heightened preparedness / anticipatory action | Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment, warning-action decisions, resource readiness, anticipatory measures and possible State of Imminent Disaster declaration. | CDRRMC coordination, Mayor''s executive authority and CDRRMO technical leadership. |\n| Emergency operations                          | Incident coordination, situational awareness, resource mobilization, policy support and operational reporting.                                         | Activated EOC, Incident Management Team and designated response clusters.         |\n\nThe Manual applies to all hazards and emergencies within the City''s jurisdiction, including hydrometeorological, geological, climate-related, technological and human-induced hazards, subject to applicable institutional mandates.\n\nIt shall be read together with the City''s approved DRRM plans, contingency plans, Public Service Continuity Plan, climate and disaster risk assessment, development plans, land-use plans and other applicable local policies.\n\n# 4. Legal, Policy and Standards Framework\n\nThe institutional arrangements shall be interpreted and implemented within the following hierarchy of legal and policy instruments.\n\n## 4.1 Philippine Legal and Regulatory Framework\n\n| **Instrument**                                                  | **Relevant Provisions**                                                                  | **Application to this Chapter**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010)                                   | Sections 11–17 and 21                                                                    | Establishment and functions of CDRRMC/CDRRMO, emergency coordination, State of Calamity and LDRRMF governance.   |\n| IRR of RA 10121                                                 | Relevant provisions on local councils, offices, coordination and declarations            | Operationalization of statutory institutional responsibilities.                                                  |\n| RA 7160 (1991) – Local Government Code                          | Sections 16, 33, 76 and relevant provisions on local legislative and executive authority | Local autonomy, inter-LGU cooperation, organizational structure and local governance.                            |\n| RA 12287 (2025) – Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act | Sections 3–7 and applicable implementing provisions                                      | Anticipatory declaration, pre-disaster risk assessment and early action.                                         |\n| IRR of RA 12287 (2026)                                          | Rules II–VII                                                                             | Local declaration procedures, anticipatory action, funding, capacity development and post-declaration reporting. |\n| RA 9729 (2009), as amended by RA 10174                          | Climate change adaptation and financing provisions                                       | Integration of climate resilience into DRRM governance and planning.                                             |\n| RA 10173 (2012) – Data Privacy Act                              | Personal information processing and protection                                           | Protection of affected-population, beneficiary, responder and institutional information.                         |\n| RA 9710 (2009) – Magna Carta of Women                           | Gender-responsive governance                                                             | Gender-responsive and inclusive institutional arrangements.                                                      |\n| RA 7277, as amended                                             | Rights and accessibility of persons with disabilities                                    | Accessible governance, warning and emergency services.                                                           |\n| RA 9994 (2010)                                                  | Senior citizen protection                                                                | Inclusion of older persons in DRRM decision-making and service arrangements.                                     |\n| RA 10821 (2016)                                                 | Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act                                           | Child protection and child-sensitive emergency arrangements.                                                     |\n| RA 11036 (2018) – Mental Health Act                             | Mental health services and protection                                                    | Institutional and responder psychosocial support.                                                                |\n| RA 12076 (2024) – Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act                      | Standards for permanent evacuation centers                                               | Institutional coordination on evacuation infrastructure and facility compliance.                                 |\n| RA 12009 (2024) – New Government Procurement Act                | Government procurement and applicable emergency procurement procedures                   | Resource governance and procurement accountability.                                                              |\n\nThe Local Government Code and RA 10121 provide the foundation for the City''s organizational and disaster governance responsibilities. RA 12287 introduces an additional anticipatory governance mechanism that must be integrated into existing DRRM processes rather than treated as a separate emergency procedure.\n\n## 4.2 Administrative and Operational Guidelines\n\nThe following administrative and operational instruments shall guide institutional implementation:\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                    | **Application**                                                                                                   |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| NDRRMC-DILG-DBM-CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                                | LDRRMO organizational structure, staffing and functions.                                                          |\n| NDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1                                                    | LDRRMF programming and utilization.                                                                               |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002                                                         | Accounting and reporting of the LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund.                                                    |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012                                         | Institutionalization and use of the Incident Command System.                                                      |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018, as identified in the assessment      | EOC establishment, operationalization and management; issuance details require confirmation with OCD Region VIII. |\n| NDCC Memorandum Circular No. 12, s. 2008                                          | Cluster-based disaster response coordination.                                                                     |\n| RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006 and DILG VIII memorandum dated 26 February 2025 | Harmonized pre-disaster, during-disaster, post-disaster and terminal reporting.                                   |\n| OCD/NDRRMC Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook                           | Continuity of essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate arrangements and succession.               |\n| DILG Operation L!sto and applicable annual issuances                              | Preparedness and institutional readiness of LGUs and barangays.                                                   |\n\nJMC No. 2014-1 specifically recognizes the LDRRMO as a mandatory office and provides for the Head and three core functional areas: Administration and Training, Research and Planning, and Operations and Warning. It also permits appropriate augmentation of staffing and organizational arrangements, subject to the applicable requirements.\n\n## 4.3 International Frameworks and Management Standards\n\nThe MOO shall also be guided by the following frameworks:\n\n| **Framework/Standard**                                                            | **Application to Institutional Governance**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030                            | Strengthening disaster risk governance, understanding risk, investing in resilience and enhancing preparedness and recovery. |\n| UNDRR Making Cities Resilient 2030 – Ten Essentials                               | Alignment with the City''s urban resilience governance and institutional performance.                                         |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Diagnostic reference for institutional and city-wide resilience assessment.                                                  |\n| ISO 31000:2018 – Risk Management Guidelines                                       | Integrating risk identification, analysis, evaluation, treatment, monitoring and communication into governance.              |\n| ISO 9001:2015 – Quality Management Systems                                        | Process ownership, documented procedures, performance evaluation, corrective action and continual improvement.               |\n| ISO 22301:2019 – Business Continuity Management Systems                           | Continuity of essential services, organizational resilience and disruption management.                                       |\n| Sphere Handbook (2018)                                                            | Humanitarian quality and accountability principles for emergency assistance.                                                 |\n| CREST / systems-thinking approaches                                               | Understanding interdependencies, feedback loops, cascading impacts and system-level interventions.                           |\n\nThese frameworks are complementary rather than substitutes for Philippine laws and regulations. Where a standard or international framework is more demanding than the applicable statutory minimum, the City may adopt it as a good-practice benchmark, subject to its authority and resources.\n\n## 4.4 Policy Hierarchy and Conflict Resolution\n\nIn implementing this chapter, the following order shall apply:\n\n1.  Philippine Constitution and applicable statutes;\n\n2.  Valid implementing rules and regulations;\n\n3.  National administrative and operational issuances;\n\n4.  Provincial and regional coordination requirements, where applicable;\n\n5.  City ordinances and valid executive issuances;\n\n6.  Approved plans, policies and institutional procedures;\n\n7.  MOO protocols, checklists and supporting forms.\n\nIn case of inconsistency, the higher-ranking legal or regulatory instrument shall prevail. The CDRRMO shall refer material legal ambiguities to the City Legal Office and seek technical clarification from OCD Region VIII or the appropriate national agency.\n\nThe MOO shall maintain a legal and policy register, with an identified custodian and review date for each applicable instrument.\n\n# 5. Systems-Thinking Framework for DRRM Governance\n\n## 5.1 Institutional System Architecture\n\nOrmoc City''s DRRM governance shall operate as a connected system comprising five interdependent layers:\n\n1.  **Executive governance**: Political leadership, statutory authority and executive decision-making.\n\n2.  **Policy and oversigh**t: CDRRMC deliberation, policy direction, recommendations and monitoring.\n\n3.  **Institutional management**: CDRRMO planning, technical management, administration and implementation.\n\n4.  **Operational coordination**: EOC, ICS, response clusters and incident management arrangements.\n\n5.  **Community and external interface:** BDRRMCs, CSOs, private-sector institutions, utilities, national agencies and neighbouring LGUs.\n\n**Integrated CDRRMO–EOC Governance System**\n\n![Figure 3: 5.1 Institutional System Architecture](/library/ch1v12/fig-03.webp)\n\n**Conceptual relationship**. This is not a substitute for the statutory chain of authority or the approved ICS organizational chart.\n\nThe architecture recognizes that information, authority and resources move through different pathways.\n\n- Executive authority flows through legally established government structures.\n\n- Technical information flows from monitoring institutions, technical offices and communities toward decision-makers.\n\n- Operational instructions flow through the approved command and coordination arrangements.\n\n- Resources flow through authorized mobilization and accountability mechanisms.\n\n- Feedback and performance information flow back into planning, policy and institutional improvement.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall ensure that these pathways are connected but not conflated.\n\n## 5.2 The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface\n\nThe assessment identifies the institutional interface as the weakest component, with a separate maturity index of 1.41/4.00.\n\nThis indicates that the problem is not limited to individual office capacity. It concerns the connections between institutional actors, their processes, information systems and decision-making arrangements.\n\nThe following operating distinctions shall therefore apply:\n\n| **Dimension**            | **CDRRMO**                                                                                     | **EOC**                                                                                   |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional status     | Permanent LGU office                                                                           | Operational coordination mechanism established through appropriate local issuance         |\n| Operating condition      | Continuous normal operations                                                                   | Activated according to approved triggers and authority                                    |\n| Primary accountability   | CDRRMO Head                                                                                    | Designated EOC Manager                                                                    |\n| Main function            | DRRM governance, planning, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery support | Incident coordination, information management, resource coordination and decision support |\n| Planning horizon         | Strategic, annual and multi-year                                                               | Incident-specific and operational-period based                                            |\n| Information              | Maintains institutional risk and readiness information                                         | Consolidates and validates current incident information                                   |\n| Relationship with ICS    | Provides institutional support and coordinates preparedness arrangements                       | Supports the Incident Commander and Incident Management Team                              |\n| Institutional continuity | Maintains permanent office functions                                                           | Operates under activation, deactivation and continuity arrangements                       |\n\nThe EOC shall not replace the CDRRMO. Nor shall it become an alternative command structure for field response.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall retain incident-level command authority under the applicable ICS arrangements. The EOC shall facilitate coordination, provide information, mobilize support and elevate decisions requiring executive action.\n\n## 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies\n\nPart C of the assessment identifies 15 institutional interface indicators. These are deliberately excluded from the CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Maturity Index because the primary operational ownership rests with other offices or external institutions.\n\n![Figure 4: 5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies](/library/ch1v12/fig-04.webp)\n\nThe distinction is important for accountability.\n\n| **Interface Area**                                                  | **Primary Institutional Owner**                       | **CDRRMO’s Role**                                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk-informed CDP/CLUP priorities and scenarios                     | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO)           | Supply risk evidence and provide DRR/CCA technical advice.                       |\n| DRR/CCA screening of projects and budgets                           | CPDO and City Budget Office                           | Provide hazard and resilience screening inputs.                                  |\n| Damage, livelihood and ecosystem loss analysis                      | CPDO and relevant sector offices                      | Supply disaster risk and impact information.                                     |\n| Hazard-sensitive zoning and building permits                        | CPDO and Zoning Administrator                         | Provide technical hazard information and participate in review.                  |\n| EOC, evacuation center and critical-facility structural assessments | City Engineer / Building Official                     | Identify critical facilities and coordinate risk prioritization.                 |\n| Power, water and telecommunications continuity                      | Utility providers and relevant City offices           | Coordinate restoration priorities and continuity agreements.                     |\n| Transport anticipatory action                                       | City Engineer, traffic office, DPWH and port operator | Integrate transport risk information into emergency coordination.                |\n| Watershed and ecosystem protection                                  | CENRO and DENR                                        | Integrate ecosystem-related hazards and protective functions into DRRM planning. |\n| Health anticipatory action                                          | City Health Office and hospitals                      | Coordinate the Health Cluster and operational information.                       |\n| Education continuity                                                | DepEd Schools Division                                | Coordinate school preparedness and learning-continuity arrangements.             |\n| Agriculture and fisheries anticipatory action                       | City Agriculture Office and City Veterinary Office    | Coordinate hazard information and response integration.                          |\n| Pre-impact price monitoring                                         | DTI and City Market Office                            | Facilitate information sharing with the EOC.                                     |\n| Business continuity outreach                                        | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce                        | Facilitate risk communication and preparedness outreach.                         |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not assume technical or statutory responsibilities assigned to these offices. Its role is to establish the coordination mechanisms, information exchange and performance monitoring necessary for integrated city-wide resilience.\n\nThis distinction is essential to avoid institutional duplication and unfunded mandates.\n\n# 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates\n\n![Figure 5: 6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates](/library/ch1v12/fig-05.webp)\n\n## 6.1 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council\n\nThe CDRRMC shall serve as the City''s principal DRRM policy-making, coordinating and oversight body under RA 10121.\n\nIts composition and functions shall conform to Section 11 of the Act and its IRR.\n\nThe City shall maintain an updated Executive Order identifying the membership, institutional representation, designated alternates and coordination responsibilities of the Council.\n\nThe assessment (IAT v1.7, A1.2) identifies the following governance requirements:\n\n- Issuing an amending EO to EO 2024-032A, the current reorganization order;\n\n- Keeping the CSO seats (five under EO 2024-032A) supported by SP accreditation of each CSO;\n\n- Filling the vacant third private-sector seat and restoring an academe seat (the City College of Ormoc, recommended by CDRRMC Res. No. 10 s.2024);\n\n- Maintaining institutional representation rather than relying solely on individual appointments; and\n\n- Reviewing the membership register annually.\n\nThe CDRRMC shall ensure representation from relevant City Government offices, national agencies, public safety institutions, CSOs, the private sector and other prescribed members.\n\nIts principal functions shall include:\n\n1.  Approving, monitoring and evaluating the Local DRRM Plan;\n\n2.  Integrating DRR and climate change adaptation into local development plans and budgets;\n\n3.  Providing strategic direction for DRRM programs and policies;\n\n4.  Recommending pre-emptive and forced evacuation where warranted;\n\n5.  Recommending a State of Calamity declaration to the appropriate local legislative authority;\n\n6.  Coordinating anticipatory action under RA 12287;\n\n7.  Mobilizing institutional and external resources;\n\n8.  Monitoring the use of DRRM funds and other dedicated resources; and\n\n9.  Reviewing institutional performance, assessment findings and corrective actions.\n\n## 6.2 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office\n\nThe CDRRMO shall function as the City''s permanent institutional and technical mechanism for DRRM.\n\nIn accordance with Section 12 of RA 10121, it shall be responsible for setting the direction, development, implementation and coordination of local DRRM programs.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\n- Risk assessment and local risk information management;\n\n- Local DRRM planning and contingency planning;\n\n- Multi-hazard early warning;\n\n- Training, orientation and knowledge management;\n\n- Resource and capability inventory;\n\n- Coordination with communities, CSOs and private-sector partners;\n\n- Emergency monitoring and mobilization;\n\n- Organization and supervision of emergency response teams and ACDVs;\n\n- Recovery coordination;\n\n- CDRRMC Secretariat functions; and\n\n- Preparation and reporting of DRRM plans, budgets and fund utilization.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall perform these responsibilities through its approved organizational structure and in coordination with other City Government offices.\n\n## 6.3 Emergency Operations Center\n\nThe EOC shall provide the City''s centralized mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\n1.  Maintain situational awareness;\n\n2.  Consolidate, validate and disseminate incident information;\n\n3.  Support CDRRMC and executive decision-making;\n\n4.  Facilitate coordination among the Incident Management Team and response clusters;\n\n5.  Coordinate resource requests and mobilization;\n\n6.  Maintain incident and decision records;\n\n7.  Facilitate vertical coordination with provincial and regional DRRM institutions, as applicable; and\n\n8.  Support transition from response to recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall operate within the authority established by the Mayor and the approved institutional arrangements.\n\n**Scenario-based activation.** The EOC is one fixed structure, separate from the CDRRMO office chart (Figure 1.3). Each contingency plan switches on only the modules its scenario needs (Figure 1.4). CDRRMO divisions supply the section chiefs, partner offices staff the cluster desks, and the proposed cluster coordinators form the Barangay Liaison Desk. The Incident Commander commands on site; the EOC coordinates and supplies; the EOC Manager elevates policy decisions to the CDRRMC and the Mayor.\n\n**Figure 1.3**\n\n*Proposed City EOC Structure and Its Relationship With Incident Command*\n\n![Figure 6: 6.3 Emergency Operations Center](/library/ch1v12/fig-06.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed for validation at the December 2026 workshop, following NDRRMC Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018. AA = anticipatory action; IMT = incident management team; MDM = management of the dead and missing; PDRA = pre-disaster risk assessment; QRF = Quick Response Fund; WASH = water, sanitation and hygiene.\n\n**Table 1.4**\n\n*EOC Activation Levels, Staffing and Provincial Reporting Times*\n\n| **Level**      | **Who staffs the EOC**                                                                                                                                        | **Reports to the Province (DILG VIII cut-offs)** |\n|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| White (normal) | OpCen duty team, 2 per shift; EOC Manager on call.                                                                                                            | 6 AM and 6 PM                                    |\n| Blue (alert)   | Adds the EOC Manager, Planning (situation unit, PDRA and AA desk) and the Logistics lead. Cluster focal persons and the Barangay Liaison Desk are on standby. | 9 AM and 7 PM                                    |\n| Red (full)     | All four sections, command staff, cluster desks and six barangay desks; 12-hour shifts, two deep.                                                             | 9 AM and 7 PM, and as events require             |\n\n*Note.* At Red the EOC needs about 50 people over two shifts, more than the CDRRMO can field alone. Chapter 2, section 10.1 sets out how pre-designated augmentees from City offices close the gap.\n\n**Figure 1.4**\n\n*EOC Modules Activated by Contingency Scenario*\n\n![Figure 7: 6.3 Emergency Operations Center](/library/ch1v12/fig-07.webp)\n\n*Note.* Proposed; each contingency plan sets its exact triggers. "Lead clusters" means only the clusters that lead that scenario are seated. Typhoon: Blue on forecast landfall, Red at Wind Signal No. 2 or a flood or storm-surge warning; the RA 12287 path applies when lead time is 72 hours or more. Flash flood: Red on an orange or red rainfall warning; usually below the 72-hour floor, so city early action applies. Earthquake: Red automatically on strong shaking; move to the alternate EOC if the primary is damaged. El Niño: White or Blue only, coordinated by the El Niño Task Force (EO No. 2023-0042). Disease outbreak: Blue, Red at the Health Cluster''s request. Industrial incident, fire or planned event: Blue as needed, with the IMT under CDRRMC Res. No. 09 s.2024.\n\n**Geospatial common operating picture.** The EOC shall keep a geospatial common operating picture (Geo-COP) as part of its information-management function. Its reference point is the EOC location registered from the KML supplied for this Manual (11.013896° N, 124.604287° E), which the site check under IAT B2.1 used for the hazard-map overlay. The facility entrance and footprint still need a field survey. Partner offices keep ownership of their own map layers; the CDRRMO maintains the system. The Geo-COP supports coordination and does not transfer command from the Incident Commander. The full procedure (SOP 6-GIS-01) and its forms belong to the EOC Module, Chapter 6.\n\nThese arrangements address IAT items B1.4 (concept of operations), B4.1 (EOC structure), B4.4 (liaison desks) and B6.1 (ICS–EOC interface) on paper. The scores rise once the EOC Executive Order is issued and an exercise tests the structure (B7.3).\n\n## 6.4 Office of the City Mayor\n\nThe City Mayor shall exercise the powers and responsibilities vested in the Local Chief Executive under RA 7160, RA 10121, RA 12287 and other applicable laws.\n\nThe Mayor shall:\n\n- Provide executive leadership and direction;\n\n- Chair the CDRRMC;\n\n- Authorize actions within statutory authority;\n\n- Direct the mobilization of City Government offices and resources;\n\n- Issue valid executive orders and administrative directives;\n\n- Exercise the relevant declaration authority under RA 12287;\n\n- Approve institutional arrangements within the scope of executive authority; and\n\n- Elevate matters requiring legislative or higher-level government action.\n\nThe Mayor''s executive authority shall remain distinct from the legislative authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n## 6.5 Sangguniang Panlungsod\n\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise its legislative and oversight responsibilities in accordance with RA 7160 and applicable DRRM legislation.\n\nIts relevant functions include:\n\n- Enacting the ordinance establishing or strengthening the CDRRMO;\n\n- Approving the necessary organizational and budgetary measures;\n\n- Considering CDRRMC recommendations for a State of Calamity declaration;\n\n- Enacting relevant local DRRM ordinances;\n\n- Exercising oversight over local DRRM policies and resource allocation; and\n\n- Supporting the institutionalization of the City''s resilience priorities.\n\nLegal distinction: The declaration of a State of Calamity under Section 16 of RA 10121 is issued by the local sanggunian upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. It is not simply a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\n# 7. Institutional Governance Processes and Workflows\n\nThe MOO shall define governance as a series of connected, controlled and measurable processes. Each process shall have:\n\n- A defined trigger or initiating condition;\n\n- A designated process owner;\n\n- Responsible actors;\n\n- A legally authorized decision-maker;\n\n- Required inputs and evidence;\n\n- Defined outputs and records;\n\n- Escalation and exception procedures; and\n\n- Performance and compliance indicators.\n\nThis approach ensures that governance decisions can be traced from initiation to approval, implementation, reporting and review.\n\n## 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that DRRM policies, plans, priorities and institutional decisions are deliberated, approved or recommended by the appropriate authority and translated into accountable actions.\n\n![Figure 8: 7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making](/library/ch1v12/fig-08.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-01: CDRRMC Governance**\n\n1.  **Initiation and agenda development**  \n    > CDRRMO identifies policy issues, assessment findings, risk developments and matters requiring Council action.\n\n2.  **Technical preparation**  \n    > Concerned offices submit technical papers, evidence, financial implications and recommendations.\n\n3.  **Secretariat processing  \n    > **CDRRMO consolidates agenda items, issues meeting notices and circulates supporting documents.\n\n4.  **Council deliberation  \n    > **CDRRMC members review evidence, assess implications and deliberate on proposed actions.\n\n5.  **Decision and documentation**  \n    > The Council approves matters within its authority or adopts recommendations for submission to the appropriate approving authority.\n\n6.  **Implementation and assignment  \n    > **The Secretariat records decisions, assigns action owners and establishes target dates.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and feedback  \n    > **CDRRMO tracks implementation and reports unresolved actions at succeeding meetings.\n\n**Required Output**\n\nApproved minutes, resolutions or recommendations, decision register and action-monitoring report.\n\n**Operating Requirements**\n\n| **Control point**    | **Required standard**                                                                                                     |\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Meeting frequency    | At least once every three months and as necessary.                                                                        |\n| Agenda               | Include standing items on risk developments, LDRRMP implementation, funding, assessment gaps and institutional readiness. |\n| Supporting documents | Technical papers, risk information, financial implications and draft resolutions, as applicable.                          |\n| Documentation        | Minutes, attendance, decisions, dissenting views where material, assigned owners and deadlines.                           |\n| Follow-through       | Action register reviewed at every succeeding meeting.                                                                     |\n| Review               | Annual assessment of Council functionality and effectiveness.                                                             |\n\nThe assessment records a score of 4 for A1.3. This established practice should be retained and strengthened through systematic action tracking, particularly for the 30 high-priority institutional gaps.\n\n## 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that Council proceedings, decisions and institutional records are complete, traceable and accessible to authorized stakeholders.\n\n![Figure 9: 7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management](/library/ch1v12/fig-09.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-02: Secretariat Operations**\n\n1.  Receive agenda requests and supporting documents.\n\n2.  Prepare agenda, meeting notice and document package.\n\n3.  Coordinate with the Chairperson on scheduling and meeting requirements.\n\n4.  Record attendance, proceedings, resolutions and decisions.\n\n5.  Prepare draft minutes and resolutions for review and approval.\n\n6.  Distribute approved records to authorized members.\n\n7.  Enter decisions into the action register and assign responsible offices.\n\n8.  Track implementation and preserve the official records.\n\n**Required records**: Meeting calendar, agenda, attendance, minutes, resolutions, action register and document distribution log.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall develop a Secretariat SOP and use standardized templates. This responds to A1.4, which is already rated 4, while addressing the need to document the existing practice more systematically.\n\n## 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that local DRRM policies are legally sound, risk-informed, coordinated and implementable.\n\nThe process shall be used for the proposed CDRRMO enabling ordinance, updated CDRRMC Executive Order, EOC establishment issuance, ICS institutionalization and amendments to existing DRRM ordinances.\n\n![Figure 10: 7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development](/library/ch1v12/fig-10.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-03: Policy Development and Approval**\n\n1.  **Identify the policy gap.** CDRRMO documents the legal, institutional or operational deficiency.\n\n2.  **Develop the technical proposal**. Prepare the proposed policy, institutional implications and implementation requirements.\n\n3.  **Conduct consultation.** Engage affected City offices, CDRRMC members, CSOs and relevant partners.\n\n4.  **Legal review.** The City Legal Office reviews legality, authority, consistency and enforceability.\n\n5.  **Resource review**. HRMO and Budget Office assess staffing, funding and administrative implications.\n\n6.  **Approva**l. Submit the proposal to the Mayor or Sangguniang Panlungsod, depending on the nature of the instrument.\n\n7.  **Publication and dissemination**. Circulate the approved policy to responsible offices and affected stakeholders.\n\n8.  **Implementation and evaluation**. Monitor compliance, assess effectiveness and recommend revisions.\n\n**Decision rule**: The appropriate approving authority shall be determined by the legal nature of the proposed instrument. An ordinance shall not be substituted by an Executive Order where legislative action is required.\n\n## 7.4 Process 4: State of Calamity Declaration\n\nThe City shall maintain a documented protocol consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\n**Practice to date (IAT v1.7, A1.6).** The SP adopted a State of Calamity for Typhoon Odette (SP Res. No. 2021-366, December 2021). The CDRRMC recommended a State of Calamity for the 2024 dengue outbreak for a period not exceeding one year, liftable earlier on the Council''s recommendation (CDRRMC Res. No. 06 s.2024). These cases show the process in use. They also show the gaps this protocol closes: no written criteria, no standard recommendation package, and Quick Response Fund resolutions that do not cite the declaration relied on.\n\nA State of Calamity declaration is based on the results of damage assessment and needs analysis. It shall be distinguished from the anticipatory declaration mechanism under RA 12287.\n\n**Workflow GOV-04: State of Calamity**\n\n1.  **Incident assessment**  \n    > BDRRMCs, response agencies and the CDRRMO report casualties, affected populations, infrastructure damage, livelihood disruption and service interruptions.\n\n2.  **RDANA consolidation**  \n    > CDRRMO coordinates the validation and consolidation of damage and needs information.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation  \n    > **CDRRMO prepares the assessment and submits it to the CDRRMC.\n\n4.  **CDRRMC deliberation**  \n    > The Council evaluates the evidence and adopts a recommendation where the legal criteria are met.\n\n5.  **Legislative declaration  \n    > **The Sangguniang Panlungsod considers and issues the declaration or lifting through the appropriate resolution.\n\n6.  **Implementation  \n    > **The City Government implements applicable remedial measures, resource arrangements, assistance and reporting requirements.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and lifting  \n    > **The CDRRMO monitors continuing impacts and prepares the technical basis for recommending lifting when appropriate.\n\n**Required Outputs**\n\nRDANA, CDRRMC recommendation, Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution, implementation records and lifting documentation.\n\nThe City shall maintain the following supporting records:\n\n- Technical assessment and recommendation;\n\n- RDANA and other supporting reports;\n\n- CDRRMC resolution;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution;\n\n- Post-declaration action register;\n\n- Resource utilization and accountability records; and\n\n- Lifting recommendation and corresponding resolution.\n\nThe protocol shall also address coordination where national or regional declarations, infrastructure closures or emergency restrictions affect Ormoc City without automatically constituting a local State of Calamity declaration.\n\nEvery resolution that uses the Quick Response Fund shall cite the declaration relied on: the SP resolution number and date, or the Proclamation number and date for a national or regional declaration. The reading of the QRF trigger shall be validated with the City Legal Office and COA.\n\n## 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action\n\n**New statutory Governance Process**\n\nRA 12287 establishes a mechanism for declaring a State of Imminent Disaster before a disaster occurs, based on the applicable forecast, pre-disaster risk assessment and legal criteria.\n\nThe IRR released on 29 September 2026 provides the implementing framework for this mechanism.\n\nThe assessment identifies A1.8 as a mandatory requirement with a score of 1. The City has no localized procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template documented in the evidence reviewed.\n\nThe following workflow shall therefore be institutionalized.\n\n![Figure 11: 7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action](/library/ch1v12/fig-11.webp)\n\n**Workflow GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster**\n\n1.  **Hazard forecast and trigger monitoring  \n    > **CDRRMO Operations and Warning monitors official forecasts, advisories and hazard-specific triggers from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent agencies.\n\n2.  **Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment  \n    > **CDRRMO coordinates the assessment of exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, likely impacts, affected sectors, lead time and available response capacity.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation**  \n    > CDRRMO prepares the PDRA findings and proposed anticipatory measures for CDRRMC consideration.\n\n4.  **Regional coordination and recommendation  \n    > **The City coordinates with RDRRMC VIII through OCD Region VIII and submits the prescribed request and supporting documentation.\n\n5.  **Regional decision and response**  \n    > RDRRMC VIII undertakes the action required under the Act and IRR. The request, acknowledgment and prescribed response timeline are documented.\n\n6.  **Local declaration**  \n    > Upon the applicable recommendation, the City Mayor may declare a State of Imminent Disaster through an Executive Order covering the jurisdiction forecast to be affected.\n\n7.  **Activation of anticipatory measures**  \n    > Authorized offices implement approved early actions, including pre-positioning, evacuation, protection of critical infrastructure and targeted social assistance.\n\n8.  **Funding and accountability  \n    > **Budget, Accounting, Procurement and implementing offices document commitments, disbursements, utilization and outputs in accordance with the IRR and applicable DBM-COA rules.\n\n9.  **Lifting and post-declaration reporting  \n    > **The City observes the applicable lifting procedures and submits the required post-lifting report to OCD within the prescribed 45-day period.\n\nThe process shall observe the following safeguards:\n\n1.  A forecast alone shall not automatically trigger a declaration. The applicable legal criteria and PDRA requirements must be met.\n\n2.  The CDRRMO shall provide technical recommendations, not independently exercise the declaration authority.\n\n3.  The local declaration shall follow the recommendation and coordination mechanism prescribed by RA 12287 and its IRR.\n\n4.  Anticipatory actions shall be proportionate to the projected impacts, hazard lead time, available resources and applicable legal authority.\n\n5.  Measures shall observe inclusion, protection, do-no-harm and no-regrets principles.\n\n6.  The use of funds shall follow the statutory funding limits, tranche arrangements and applicable financial regulations.\n\n7.  Decisions, approvals, actions and expenditures shall be documented for audit and evaluation.\n\n**Funding control**: As reflected in the assessment workbook, the IRR provides for an anticipatory-action allocation of up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component and a 30%/70% tranche arrangement associated with the prescribed three-day lead-time rule. The precise timing, eligibility, disbursement and documentation requirements must be implemented using the certified IRR and applicable DBM-COA guidance. The MOO shall not treat this allocation as an additional fund outside the statutory LDRRMF framework.\n\n**Independent Component City clarification:** Ormoc''s status as an Independent Component City must be considered when establishing its vertical coordination arrangements. The City shall obtain written confirmation from OCD Region VIII on the application of the IRR''s regional and provincial coordination provisions, particularly whether any provincial declaration has legal applicability to Ormoc. The City''s ICC status shall not be used as a reason to bypass the RDRRMC recommendation mechanism expressly established by RA 12287.\n\n## 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination\n\nInstitutional escalation shall occur when:\n\n- The scale of an incident exceeds the capacity of a barangay;\n\n- Multiple barangays require coordinated intervention;\n\n- City resources are insufficient;\n\n- Specialized national or regional assistance is required;\n\n- Critical infrastructure or essential services are disrupted; or\n\n- A statutory declaration or executive decision is necessary.\n\nRA 10121, Section 15, provides the general coordination framework: barangay-level leadership where a barangay is affected, city/municipal leadership where two or more barangays are affected, and progressively higher coordination levels when impacts exceed the relevant territorial scale. Higher councils and institutions support the LGU''s primary response responsibility.\n\n![Figure 12: 7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination](/library/ch1v12/fig-12.webp)\n\nThe operational workflow shall be:\n\n1.  Incident or risk information is reported to the appropriate local authority.\n\n2.  The affected BDRRMC undertakes initial action and reports to the City.\n\n3.  CDRRMO validates the information and assesses the level of support required.\n\n4.  The CDRRMC and EOC coordinate City-wide institutional action, as appropriate.\n\n5.  The City requests external assistance through the prescribed channels when required.\n\n6.  The receiving institution acknowledges and assigns the request.\n\n7.  The CDRRMO monitors the response and maintains the institutional record.\n\n8.  Unresolved requirements are escalated through the appropriate higher-level coordination mechanism.\n\n## 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action\n\nAll governance processes shall be subject to periodic review.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\n1.  Consolidate compliance and performance information;\n\n2.  Compare actual results against approved targets;\n\n3.  Identify gaps, recurring problems and systemic causes;\n\n4.  Assign corrective actions to accountable offices;\n\n5.  Establish completion dates and required resources;\n\n6.  Verify implementation;\n\n7.  Report unresolved issues to the CDRRMC; and\n\n8.  Update policies and procedures when necessary.\n\nThis process shall link the Institutional Assessment Tool, Action Plan, CDRRMC action register, annual LDRRMP monitoring and the MOO revision mechanism.\n\n![Figure 13: 7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action](/library/ch1v12/fig-13.webp)\n\n# 8. Responsibility Assignment and RACI Framework\n\n## 8.1 Application\n\nThe RACI framework shall be used to clarify institutional responsibility for governance processes and prevent overlapping mandates, unassigned tasks and ambiguous decision authority.\n\nThe following definitions apply:\n\n| **Code** | **Responsibility** | **Application**                                                               |\n|----------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| R        | Responsible        | Performs the work and produces the required output.                           |\n| A        | Accountable        | Owns the result and approves the output or decision. Only one A per activity. |\n| **C**    | Consulted          | Provides technical, legal or operational advice before the decision.          |\n| **I**    | Informed           | Receives relevant information on the decision or outcome.                     |\n\nRACI assignments shall be interpreted subject to statutory powers, formal delegations and approved organizational arrangements. A RACI designation does not create authority that does not otherwise exist under law.\n\n## 8.2 Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix\n\n| **Role**                             | **Abbreviation** | **Principal Responsibility**                                                        |\n|--------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Local Chief Executive                | Mayor            | Executive authority and statutory decisions.                                        |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod               | SP               | Legislative approval and local declaration authority.                               |\n| CDRRMC                               | Council          | Collective policy direction, deliberation and recommendation.                       |\n| CDRRMO Head                          | CDRRMO           | Permanent office leadership and technical accountability.                           |\n| CDRRMO Secretariat                   | Secretariat      | Council administration, documentation and action tracking.                          |\n| City Legal Office                    | CLO              | Legal review and compliance advice.                                                 |\n| City Planning and Development Office | CPDO             | Integration of DRR/CCA into development planning.                                   |\n| HRMO                                 | HRMO             | Staffing, qualification and personnel administration.                               |\n| City Budget / Accounting / BAC       | Finance          | Budgeting, accounting, procurement and financial control.                           |\n| City Information Office              | CIO              | Public information and risk communication support.                                  |\n| BDRRMCs                              | Barangays        | Community-level governance, reporting and local action.                             |\n| City departments and agencies        | Departments      | Sectoral implementation and technical support.                                      |\n| OCD / RDRRMC VIII                    | Regional         | Regional coordination, technical assistance and statutory recommendation functions. |\n| EOC Manager / IMT                    | EOC/IMT          | Emergency coordination and incident-management interface.                           |\n| CSOs / Private Sector                | Partners         | Community participation, technical support and resource commitments.                |\n\n## 8.3 Master RACI Matrix – institutional Governance\n\nThe following matrix establishes the proposed responsibility assignments for Chapter 1.\n\n| **Governance activity**                                   | **Mayor** | **SP** | **CDRRMC** | **CDRRMO** | **Secretariat** | **CLO** | **HRMO / Finance** | **OCD VIII** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------|------------|------------|-----------------|---------|--------------------|--------------|\n| Establish CDRRMO through ordinance                        | I         | A      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC composition EO                             | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Maintain CDRRMC membership register                       | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC agenda and meetings                        | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | I       | I                  | I            |\n| Document Council resolutions                              | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Monitor Council decisions                                 | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | I       | **C**              | I            |\n| Develop local DRRM policy                                 | A         | **C**  | **C**      | R          | **C**           | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Review proposed DRRM ordinances                           | **C**     | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | A       | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Prepare State of Calamity technical assessment            | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Recommend State of Calamity                               | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Issue local State of Calamity declaration                 | I         | A      | R          | C          | I               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare State of Imminent Disaster PDRA                   | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Submit request to RDRRMC VIII                             | I         | I      | A          | R          | **C**           | **C**   | I                  | **C**        |\n| Recommend regional State of Imminent Disaster Declaration | I         | I      | **C**      | **C**      | I               | I       | I                  | A/R\\*        |\n| Issue local State of Imminent Disaster EO                 | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Coordinate anticipatory action                            | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | R                  | **C**        |\n| Prepare 45-day post-lifting report                        | I         | I      | **C**      | A/R        | **C**           | **C**   | R                  | I            |\n| Maintain legal and policy register                        | I         | I      | I          | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Conduct annual governance review                          | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n\n\\*The RACI assignment for OCD/RDRRMC VIII reflects the regional recommendation function described in RA 12287. The exact responsible regional body and administrative sequence shall be finalized against the certified IRR and applicable regional procedures. The RACI matrix does not replace the statutory decision authority of the NDRRMC, regional councils, Mayor or Sanggunian.\n\n## 8.4 RACI Matrix – institutional interfaces\n\nPart C of the assessment shall be operationalized through the following responsibility arrangements.\n\n| **Interface activity**                  | **Accountable owner**                        | **Responsible actors**             | **CDRRMO role**                                 |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRA and CLUP integration               | CPDO                                         | CPDO technical team                | C / technical input                             |\n| DRR/CCA project screening               | CPDO                                         | CPDO, City Budget Office           | C / risk validation                             |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official            | Engineering and inspection teams   | C / risk prioritization                         |\n| Utility continuity agreements           | Relevant utility / designated City authority | Utility providers and City offices | R for coordination, subject to approved mandate |\n| Watershed and ecosystem monitoring      | CENRO                                        | CENRO, DENR and partners           | C / DRRM integration                            |\n| Health anticipatory action              | City Health Officer                          | CHO, hospitals and health partners | C / coordination                                |\n| Education anticipatory action           | DepEd Schools Division                       | School DRRM coordinators           | C / coordination                                |\n| Agriculture and fisheries early action  | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office         | CAO, CVO and relevant agencies     | C / coordination                                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch1_v1.2.md (Chapter 1, v1.2 working draft (IAT v1.5))','draft')
